Entry-controlled vs Exit-controlled loops

1. Entry-controlled loop — while

Condition is checked before executing the loop body.

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int i = 5;

    while (i < 5)
    {
        printf("Hello");
        i++;
    }

    return 0;
}

Here, i < 5 is false at the start, so the loop runs 0 times.


---

2. Exit-controlled loop — do-while

Condition is checked after executing the loop body.

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int i = 5;

    do
    {
        printf("Hello");
        i++;
    } while (i < 5);

    return 0;
}

Here, i < 5 is false at the start, but the loop still runs 1 time because do-while executes the body first.

